window.FOUNT_WHY={"745":"Central = brain and spinal cord; peripheral = all the nerves branching out to the body.","746":"The CNS is the control centre: brain plus spinal cord.","747":"The PNS splits into somatic (voluntary) and autonomic (automatic).","748":"Somatic = body movements you choose to make.","749":"Autonomic = automatic, things you don't think about.","751":"Digestion is automatic and happens at rest, so it's controlled by the parasympathetic division.","752":"Reflexes skip the brain, going through the spinal cord to save time.","755":"Fight or flight gets the body ready to face a threat or run.","756":"The hypothalamus spots the threat and sets off the response.","757":"Adrenaline is the fight or flight hormone.","758":"Adrenaline comes from the adrenal glands, on top of the kidneys.","759":"Everything speeds up for action; digestion slows.","760":"More blood means more oxygen for the muscles.","761":"Digestion isn't urgent, so energy goes to the muscles.","762":"Adrenaline is a hormone in the blood and a neurotransmitter at synapses.","764":"James-Lange: event → body reacts → we notice it → emotion.","765":"The body reacts before we feel the emotion.","766":"'My heart is racing, so I must be scared': arousal comes first.","768":"Schachter and Singer added thinking: we use the situation to label our arousal.","770":"It was based on observations, not experiments, so it lacks scientific evidence.","771":"A neuron is a nerve cell carrying messages.","773":"Sensory neurons carry messages from the senses to the CNS.","774":"Relay neurons connect neurons within the CNS.","775":"Motor neurons carry messages to muscles and glands.","776":"Motor neurons reach far out to muscles, so they need long axons.","777":"Sensory neurons have long dendrites reaching out to the senses.","778":"Relay neurons work locally, so both their axons and dendrites are short.","779":"Dendrites receive messages.","780":"The nucleus is in the cell body.","781":"The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body.","782":"Myelin insulates the axon and speeds up the impulse.","783":"The impulse jumps between the gaps in the myelin, called nodes of Ranvier.","784":"Axon terminals release neurotransmitters.","785":"Motor neurons end at a motor end plate on the muscle.","786":"A neuron firing is an action potential.","787":"Along a neuron, the message is electrical.","788":"Across the gap between neurons, it's chemical (neurotransmitters).","789":"The gap between neurons is the synapse.","790":"Passing a message across the synapse is synaptic transmission.","791":"Neurotransmitters are chemical messengers.","792":"Electrical impulse → neurotransmitters released → cross the gap → bind to receptors.","793":"Vesicles are tiny sacs holding neurotransmitters.","794":"Receptor sites are where neurotransmitters bind on the next neuron.","797":"Adrenaline excites (makes firing more likely); serotonin inhibits (less likely).","798":"Summation adds up excitatory and inhibitory signals to decide whether the next neuron fires.","799":"Hebb: neurons that are used together strengthen their connections.","800":"An engram is a memory trace in the brain.","801":"Practice strengthens the engram.","802":"A cell assembly is a group of neurons working together.","803":"Repeated firing grows the connection: neuronal growth.","804":"Hebb was the first to show the brain can change: plasticity.","805":"Hebb ignores motivation and environment, so the theory is reductionist.","806":"Hebb's theory shows practice builds learning, which helps teaching.","808":"The motor area is at the back of the frontal lobe.","809":"The motor area controls movements you choose to make.","810":"The somatosensory area, in the parietal lobe, processes touch.","811":"The visual area is in the occipital lobe, at the back.","812":"The auditory area is in the temporal lobe, by the ears.","813":"Broca's area (speech production) is in the left frontal lobe.","816":"Wernicke's area (understanding language) is in the left temporal lobe.","817":"Tan could only say 'tan'; Broca found damage to the left frontal lobe.","818":"Aphasia is language difficulty caused by brain damage.","821":"The frontal lobe is for planning, decisions and controlling behaviour.","822":"The interpretive cortex is where memories and our interpretations of them are stored.","823":"The interpretive cortex is in the temporal lobe.","824":"Penfield stimulated awake patients' brains and recorded what they reported.","825":"Penfield studied over 1,000 patients, all with epilepsy.","826":"Patients had to be awake to say what they experienced.","827":"Stimulating the temporal lobe brought back vivid memories.","828":"Only about 8% of patients showed these memory responses.","829":"Specific responses from one area support localisation of function.","830":"Patients described their experiences in words: qualitative.","831":"Electrical stimulation isn't how the brain normally works, so it lacks ecological validity.","832":"Epileptic brains may not be typical.","833":"Penfield's work led to the Montreal Procedure for treating epilepsy.","834":"Cognitive psychology studies mental processes like memory and perception.","835":"Neuroscience studies the brain and nervous system.","836":"Cognitive neuroscience links mental processes to brain structures.","837":"Linking brain chemicals or areas to behaviour is cognitive neuroscience in action.","838":"A stroke cuts off the blood supply to part of the brain.","839":"Damage to Broca's area causes speech production problems.","840":"Neurological damage is damage to the nervous system.","841":"Each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body.","842":"CT, PET and fMRI are the three types of scan.","843":"A CT scan combines X-rays from many angles into 'slices'.","844":"CT shows structure only, not activity.","845":"PET uses an injected radioactive tracer that shows active areas.","846":"PET measures metabolic activity.","847":"On a PET scan, red is most active and blue least.","848":"Radiation limits how many PET scans someone can safely have.","849":"fMRI uses magnets to detect oxygen-rich blood in active areas.","850":"The haemodynamic response (blood flow) is what fMRI measures.","851":"BOLD = Blood Oxygen Levels Detected.","852":"More oxygen in an area means it's more active.","853":"Blood flow lags behind brain activity by a few seconds.","854":"fMRI uses no radiation and shows activity in detail.","855":"Scans are expensive and only show links, not causes.","856":"Tulving's was the 'gold' memory study (1989).","857":"Tulving used PET with radioactive gold.","858":"Only 6 participants, including Tulving and his wife.","859":"Tulving tested whether episodic and semantic memories are stored in different places.","860":"Each participant did 8 trials: 4 episodic and 4 semantic.","861":"The IV was the type of memory thought about.","862":"The DV was blood flow (brain activity).","863":"Episodic memories showed up at the front of the brain.","864":"Semantic memories showed up at the back.","865":"Different areas lit up, so the two memory types are stored separately.","866":"Six people, including the researcher, is tiny and possibly biased.","867":"PET scans give objective, scientific data.","868":"The named Brain studies are Penfield and Tulving.","1038":"Excitation makes the next neuron more likely to fire.","1039":"Inhibition makes the next neuron less likely to fire.","1040":"Localised function: specific areas do specific jobs.","1041":"Motor neurons carry messages from the CNS to the muscles.","1042":"Neurons that fire together grow their connections.","1044":"The occipital lobe, at the back, processes vision.","1045":"The parietal lobe, at the top, processes touch, heat and pain.","1046":"The PNS connects the CNS to the rest of the body.","1047":"Relay neurons link sensory and motor neurons in the CNS.","1048":"Sensory neurons carry information from the senses to the CNS.","1049":"The somatic system handles senses and voluntary movement.","1050":"The somatosensory area processes touch and pain.","1051":"A synapse is the gap between two neurons.","1052":"The temporal lobe processes hearing and language.","1053":"The thalamus relays sensory information to the cortex.","1346":"Pulling away before feeling pain is a reflex through the spinal cord.","1349":"Choosing to move is the somatic system.","1350":"Body first ('heart races'), then emotion ('I'm scared'): James-Lange.","1351":"Practice strengthens the connections between neurons.","1352":"Understanding speech but struggling to produce it points to Broca's area.","1353":"Fluent speech that makes no sense points to Wernicke's area.","1354":"The left side of the body is processed by the right parietal lobe.","1355":"Vision is processed in the occipital lobe.","1356":"The cerebellum controls balance and coordination.","1357":"CT is good for structure, like a tumour.","1358":"fMRI shows activity without radiation.","1359":"PET shows which areas are active.","1360":"CT is quick and shows clear structure.","1361":"CT uses X-rays and shows no activity.","1362":"PET uses radiation, is costly and less detailed than fMRI.","1363":"fMRI is expensive, noisy and enclosed.","1364":"The IV was the brain area stimulated.","1365":"The DV was what patients reported.","1366":"Patients having surgery may have felt unable to refuse.","1367":"The procedure was repeated on over 1,000 patients: reliable.","1368":"Few responded, and all had epilepsy.","1369":"Participants were injected with a radioactive substance.","1370":"Tulving and his wife knew the aim, so there's possible bias.","1371":"Scans help diagnose damage and understand illness.","1372":"Fight or flight prepares us to survive danger.","1373":"Exams aren't physical threats, so the response is wasted and stressful.","1374":"Sympathetic = action mode: the pupils widen.","1375":"Parasympathetic = rest mode: heart rate slows.","1376":"Action mode reduces saliva, giving a dry mouth.","1377":"Tan and brain scans support localisation of function.","1378":"Many tasks use several areas, and the brain can reorganise after damage.","1379":"Plasticity: the brain can change and form new connections.","1380":"New connections mean lost skills can be relearned through practice.","1381":"The hypothalamus detects the threat and switches on the sympathetic system.","507":"Brain development starts very early, in week 3, when the neural tube forms.","508":"In week 4 the neural tube splits into the spinal cord and three brain sections.","509":"In week 6 the forebrain divides into the cortex and thalamus.","510":"The cerebellum (balance and movement) has formed by week 15.","511":"The cortex folds late in pregnancy, which gives the brain its wrinkly look.","512":"The brain stem is the most developed part at birth, because it keeps us alive.","513":"Breathing and heartbeat are needed from day one, so the brain stem develops first.","514":"The brain stem runs automatic (autonomic) functions.","515":"Autonomic = automatic: breathing, heart rate and digestion happen without thinking.","516":"The cerebellum co-ordinates senses with movement.","517":"Sensorimotor means linking what we sense to how we move.","518":"Cerebellum is Latin for 'little brain'.","519":"As well as movement and balance, the cerebellum plays a part in language and emotion.","520":"The thalamus is a relay station, sending sensory information to the right part of the cortex.","521":"The cortex is also called the cerebral cortex or cerebrum.","522":"The cortex has two halves: the left and right hemispheres.","523":"Thinking happens in the cortex, the outer layer of the brain.","524":"The frontal cortex matures last, into the twenties.","525":"The four lobes are frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital.","526":"Occipital = vision, at the back of the head.","527":"Frontal = thinking and planning, at the front.","528":"Temporal = hearing, by the temples and ears.","529":"Parietal = touch, at the top.","530":"Nature = genes; nurture = environment and experience.","533":"Identical twins have the same genes, so any differences must come from the environment.","534":"Nicotine reduces brain growth in the womb.","535":"Rubella (German measles) in pregnancy can damage hearing, sight and the brain.","536":"Edwards' syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 18.","537":"An extra chromosome is genetic, so Edwards' syndrome supports nature.","538":"Babies sucked more to hear the story read to them before birth.","539":"Babies recognised a story heard in the womb, so the environment affects the brain before birth.","540":"Rats with toys and company grew bigger brains: environment matters.","541":"Rat brains aren't human brains, so the results may not apply to people.","542":"Newborns have had little experience, so what they can do is likely to be innate.","543":"Cognitive development is how thinking changes as children grow.","544":"The stage theory is Piaget's.","545":"A schema is a mental framework built from experience.","546":"Equilibrium is when new information fits existing schemas, so nothing needs changing.","547":"The order is sensorimotor → pre-operational → concrete → formal.","548":"Sensorimotor, from birth to 2.","549":"Pre-operational, from 2 to 7.","550":"Concrete operational, from 7 to 11.","551":"Formal operational, from 11 onwards.","552":"Babies learn by sensing and moving.","554":"Object permanence develops at around 8 months.","555":"Searching for a hidden toy shows the baby knows it still exists.","556":"Symbolic thinking means using one thing to stand for another, like a banana phone.","557":"Egocentrism means only seeing your own point of view.","558":"Young children picked their own view of the mountains.","559":"Conservation: the amount stays the same even if it looks different.","560":"Changing how something looks (taller glass, longer row) and asking if the amount changed.","561":"Conservation comes at the concrete operational stage (7–11).","562":"Concrete thinkers can use logic, but only with real objects.","563":"Abstract thinking comes at the formal operational stage (11+).","564":"Hypothetical thinking means reasoning about 'what if' situations.","565":"The policeman doll study was Hughes (1975).","566":"Hiding from the policemen means seeing their view, which tests egocentrism.","567":"The mountains task made little sense to young children.","568":"Hiding a boy from policemen needs the child to take the policemen's view.","569":"90% of young children succeeded, far more than in Piaget's mountains task.","570":"With a task that makes sense, young children can decentre.","571":"An 'accidental' change by naughty teddy, instead of a deliberate one by the adult.","572":"Naughty teddy tested conservation of number.","573":"80 children aged 4–6 took part.","574":"When teddy did it, 62% conserved, compared with 16% when the adult did it.","575":"Young children conserved earlier than Piaget said, so he underestimated them.","576":"Each child did both versions, so it was repeated measures.","577":"A deliberate change by the adult hinted that the answer should change.","578":"Studying his own children risked researcher bias.","579":"Piaget studied a few Swiss children, including his own.","580":"Many adults never reach abstract thinking.","581":"Piaget's ideas shaped education, e.g. the Plowden Report and discovery learning.","582":"Later studies found children develop earlier than Piaget said.","583":"Readiness means only teaching what a child's stage allows.","584":"Concrete aids suit concrete thinkers.","585":"Discovery learning means learning by doing and finding out.","586":"Children develop at different rates, so they should work at their own pace.","587":"The Plowden Report (1967) brought Piaget's ideas into primary schools.","588":"Bennett found discovery learning wasn't best for the basics.","589":"Practice helped children who weren't 'ready', which challenges readiness.","590":"Mindset theory is Carol Dweck's.","593":"A growth mindset sees failure as a chance to learn.","594":"A fixed mindset sees failure as proof you're not good enough.","597":"Half learned that the brain grows like a muscle; half learned study skills only.","598":"Learning about growth improved motivation and grades.","599":"One hour of growth mindset teaching raised low achievers' maths grades.","600":"Praise is approval that motivates.","602":"Praise works when it's honest, deserved and about the work.","603":"Relying on outside praise may stop students motivating themselves.","605":"Self-efficacy is Bandura's idea.","607":"Past success and feedback build self-efficacy.","608":"Learning styles are preferred ways of taking in information.","609":"Visual, auditory (verbal) and kinaesthetic.","610":"Visualisers prefer pictures and diagrams.","611":"Verbalisers prefer words: reading, listening and talking.","612":"Kinaesthetic learners prefer hands-on activities.","613":"Preferring words over pictures makes them a verbaliser.","614":"Learning styles encouraged teachers to vary their methods.","615":"There's little evidence that matching learning style improves learning.","616":"Coffield found 71 different models of learning style.","617":"Daniel Willingham criticised learning styles.","618":"Teach in the way that suits the content, e.g. maps for geography.","619":"Unexpected praise boosts internal motivation.","620":"Cued recall: build cues while learning that trigger memory later.","621":"Retrieval practice: testing yourself rather than re-reading.","622":"Delayed gratification: waiting for a bigger reward later.","623":"Waiting for a bigger reward builds self-control for long-term goals.","624":"Scans are costly, and brain differences don't tell teachers what to do.","625":"Willingham may undervalue creativity and modern teaching.","1031":"Concrete operational = logic, but only with real objects.","1032":"Formal operational = abstract, logical reasoning.","1033":"Pre-operational = symbols, but not yet logic.","1121":"Searching for the hidden toy shows object permanence.","1122":"Thinking gran sees the same view is egocentrism.","1123":"Thinking the taller glass has more means they can't conserve yet.","1124":"'What if' questions need formal operational thinking.","1125":"Logic with real objects is the concrete operational stage.","1128":"A banana as a phone is symbolic thinking.","1129":"Blocks give concrete support at the concrete stage.","1130":"Finding out by experimenting is discovery learning.","1131":"'Not a maths person' is a fixed mindset.","1132":"Believing practice helps is a growth mindset.","1133":"Praising effort encourages a growth mindset.","1134":"Praising ability can make students fear failing.","1135":"Past success breeds confidence: high self-efficacy.","1136":"Past failure lowers confidence: low self-efficacy.","1137":"Willingham: match the method to the content, not the pupil.","1138":"Testing yourself is retrieval practice.","1139":"Hiding from a policeman makes sense to children, so it measured their real ability.","1140":"30 Scottish children may not represent all children.","1141":"Hiding from policemen makes 'human sense'; mountains are abstract.","1142":"The IV was where the policemen were placed.","1143":"The DV was whether the boy was hidden correctly.","1144":"The IV was who changed the counters: the adult or teddy.","1145":"The DV was whether the child conserved.","1146":"The same children did both versions, so participant variables were controlled.","1147":"Children may have been distracted by teddy and not really conserving.","1148":"A directional hypothesis predicts more children conserve when teddy moves the counters.","1149":"Young children need parental consent.","1150":"A few Swiss children aren't representative.","1151":"Piaget's ideas can be tested with tasks like conservation.","1152":"Children don't always fit neatly into one stage.","1153":"Some subjects, like reading, need direct teaching.","1154":"Active learning matched to readiness suits each stage.","1155":"Mindset theory is easy to use in schools.","1156":"Questionnaires can be answered to look good.","1157":"Some cultures already value effort, so the effect may differ.","1158":"A small group of underachievers may not represent everyone.","1159":"The IV was which session students had.","1160":"The DV was students' motivation and grades.","1161":"Small successes and encouraging feedback build self-efficacy.","1162":"Self-efficacy relies on self-report, which is hard to measure objectively.","1163":"Willingham's ideas are based on evidence.","1164":"They may undervalue creativity.","1165":"People's preferences change, so the answers aren't consistent.","1166":"There's no good evidence for learning styles, and 71 styles is impractical.","1167":"The order in which brain structures develop is set by genes: nature.","1168":"The womb environment affects development: nurture.","1169":"An extra chromosome is genetic: nature.","1170":"Hunger or other factors could affect sucking, and the sample was small.","1171":"Babies learn sounds before they're born.","1172":"Same genes, so differences must come from the environment.","1173":"Genes and environment work together.","1174":"Moving, noisy toys build motor skills and object permanence.","1175":"Role play reduces egocentrism; cooking teaches conservation.","1176":"Abstract problems suit the formal operational stage.","1474":"An accidental-looking change let younger children show they can conserve.","1475":"Hiding games are familiar to children: high mundane realism.","1476":"Pashler found no good evidence for teaching to learning styles.","1477":"Most mindset research is from individualist cultures.","1478":"Willingham's ideas are based on scientific evidence, e.g. brain-wave studies.","1479":"Quizzes that make students recall earlier learning are retrieval practice.","6001":"Identify = just name it. No explanation needed.","6002":"Describe = say what it is or what happened, not why.","6003":"Explain = give reasons: how or why.","6004":"Evaluate = strengths and weaknesses, then a judgement.","6005":"Discuss = describe AND evaluate.","6006":"Outline = the main points, briefly.","6007":"Compare = similarities and/or differences.","6008":"Suggest = apply what you know to something new.","6009":"Justify = back it up with reasons or evidence.","6010":"Calculate = work out a number and show your working.","6011":"Distinguish between = explain how they differ.","6012":"AO1 = knowing and understanding.","6013":"AO2 = applying it to a scenario.","6014":"AO3 = analysing and evaluating.","6015":"Using knowledge to explain a person in a scenario is application: AO2.","6016":"'Describe' tests knowledge: AO1.","6017":"'Evaluate' tests AO3.","6018":"Two papers, each worth half.","6019":"Each paper is 1 hour 45 minutes.","6020":"Each paper is out of 100 marks.","6021":"105 minutes for 100 marks is about a minute per mark.","6022":"Paper 1 (Cognition and behaviour) is memory, perception, development and research methods.","6023":"Paper 2 (Social context and behaviour) is social influence, language, brain and psychological problems.","6024":"At least 10% of the marks test maths.","6025":"4 marks of description, then 3 TDEE paragraphs for 5 marks.","6026":"Full TDEE paragraphs earn the most evaluation credit.","6027":"Scenario questions need details from the scenario, or you lose marks.","6028":"1 mark to state the weakness, 1 to explain why it's a problem.","6029":"Operationalise = make it measurable.","6030":"There's no penalty for a wrong guess, so never leave it blank.","6031":"1 mark = a word or short phrase.","6032":"PEE = Point, Evidence, Explain.","6033":"Term → define ('This means') → evidence ('For example') → explain ('This is a weakness because').","6034":"Working can earn method marks even if the answer is wrong.","6035":"Describe says what; evaluate judges how good.","6036":"A full-mark strength states it AND explains why it matters.","6037":"A precise definition includes the group, the change and the pressure.","6038":"A full-mark improvement is specific AND explains why it helps.","6039":"Quote the actual numbers from the table.","6040":"A study design needs the IV, DV, design, sample, procedure and how you'll measure.","6041":"Use the last minutes to check calculations and blanks.","6042":"TDEE = Term, Define, Evidence, Explain.","6043":"Use PEE when there's no key term to define.","6044":"Define = what the term means.","6045":"Explain = why it's a strength or weakness.","6046":"4 marks describe, 5 marks evaluate.","6047":"Three developed TDEE paragraphs.","6048":"Describing a study: aim, method, results, conclusion.","6049":"Describing a theory: four separate key points.","6050":"GRAVE is for studies.","6051":"SPACER is for theories.","6052":"GRAVE = Generalisability, Reliability, Application, Validity, Ethics.","6053":"A counterargument challenges your point, usually starting 'However…'.","6054":"All four parts: term, definition, evidence, explanation.","6055":"It names the term and gives evidence but doesn't define or explain.","6056":"SPACER = Scientific, Practical applications, Alternative theories, Credibility, Ethical issues, Reductionism.","6057":"S = can it be tested objectively? C = is it believable and well supported?","6058":"Testable in labs = Scientific.","6059":"Research support = Credibility.","6060":"A real-world use = Practical applications.","6061":"Ignoring other factors = Reductionism.","6062":"Another explanation = Alternative theories.","6063":"Believable = Credibility.","6064":"Hard to test = Scientific.","6065":"Can't be measured = Scientific.","6066":"Weak supporting evidence = Credibility.","6067":"Harm from treatment = Ethical issues.","6068":"State the difference with numbers, then say what it suggests.","6069":"Full marks quotes the figures AND says what they suggest.","6070":"A bigger range means more spread: the effect varied between people.","6071":"A small sample is fixed by a larger, wider sample.","6072":"Use non-psychology students, chosen fairly.","6073":"Low ecological validity is fixed by a natural setting.","6074":"An artificial task is fixed by a realistic one.","6075":"Counterbalancing (or independent groups) fixes order effects.","6076":"Matched pairs fixes participant variables.","6077":"Professional observation avoids self-report bias.","6078":"A generic improvement isn't tied to this study.","6079":"What's changed between conditions is the IV.","6080":"What's measured is the DV.","6081":"Something else that might affect results is an extraneous variable.","6082":"'Will not affect' is a null hypothesis.","6083":"Already collected by someone else = secondary data.","6084":"A count is quantitative.","6085":"Written descriptions are qualitative.","6086":"A full-mark hypothesis is future tense, with both conditions and a measurable DV.","6087":"Past tense describes what was found, not what will happen.","6088":"Only 1 mark: it doesn't use the scenario or name a third variable.","6089":"7 ÷ 16 = 43.75%, which is 44% to 2 significant figures.","6090":"Divide both sides by 15 to get 3:1.","6091":"56.67% rounds to 57% at 2 significant figures.","6092":"40 − 29 = 11 said no, so 11/40.","6093":"Three quarters of 120 is 90, so 120 − 90 = 30 never conformed.","6094":"The total is 220, divided by 10 scores = 22.","6095":"Separate conditions (method A and B) = bar chart.","6096":"Grouped continuous scores = histogram.","6097":"Two co-variables = scatter diagram.","6098":"Histogram bars touch, and the axes and title need labels.","6099":"Name it, explain it, and give an example showing the effect.","6100":"It names the cue but doesn't point to the picture.","6101":"Quote the results, give the theory, then link them.","6102":"'How far' means saying what limits the support.","6103":"It supports Gibson, but they're animals, so it may not apply to humans.","6104":"'Method' means method only: no results.","6105":"If the question asks for one, only one gets marked.","6106":"A generic point could be about any study.","6107":"A specific point names this study's actual detail.","6108":"Points about the whole area (obedience research), with an example.","6109":"Research support increases validity.","6110":"Ignoring complexity = reductionist.","6111":"Not everyone responds the same = individual differences.","6112":"Taxi drivers' brains changed with practice, which supports Hebb.","6113":"Murdock's primacy and recency support separate STM and LTM.","6114":"Universal expressions suggest evolution, which supports Darwin.","6115":"'Whereas' makes the difference explicit.","6116":"A difference needs the same aspect compared for both.","6117":"Aversion therapy uses classical conditioning (learning by association).","6118":"Holistic = considers the whole person, including social factors.","6119":"A topic plus research methods is Type A.","6120":"A synoptic link needs both topics plus HOW one supports or challenges the other.","6121":"4 AO1 points and at least 3 developed AO3 points.","626":"Piaget: thought first, then language to describe it.","627":"Piaget says thought comes first; Sapir and Whorf say language comes first.","628":"Piaget says you need the concept (schema) before the word makes sense.","629":"Children attach words to schemas they already have.","630":"Piaget said children who repeat words without understanding are like parrots.","631":"Egocentric speech belongs to the pre-operational stage (2–7).","632":"Egocentric speech is talking from your own point of view, often to yourself.","633":"'Mummy sock' shows children understand possession before they have the grammar for it.","634":"Piaget interpreted his own observations, including of his own children, so bias is possible.","635":"Schemas can't be seen or measured, so the theory can't be proved wrong: unfalsifiable.","636":"Sapir and Whorf said language shapes thought.","637":"Sapir-Whorf: the language you speak shapes how you think.","640":"Cross-cultural = comparing different cultures.","641":"Whorf claimed that having no tense words meant the Hopi thought about time differently.","642":"One person isn't enough to generalise about a whole culture.","643":"More snow experience could come first and the words follow, which supports Piaget.","644":"Brown and Lenneberg studied the Zuni.","645":"With one word for yellow and orange, the Zuni found those colours harder to remember.","646":"Language affected colour memory, so this supports Sapir-Whorf.","647":"The Berinmo have five basic colour words.","648":"Their colour words changed where they saw boundaries, which supports Sapir-Whorf.","649":"Heider and Oliver studied the Dani.","650":"The Dani have just two colour words: roughly light/warm and dark/cool.","651":"With only two colour words, the Dani still matched colours well, so thought isn't limited by language.","652":"A non-verbal test measures thinking without relying on words.","653":"Thought worked without the words, so this supports Piaget.","654":"Cultural differences linked to language support Sapir-Whorf.","655":"Judging other cultures by English standards is ethnocentric.","656":"Elaborated = detailed and complex; restricted = short and simple (Bernstein).","657":"Simpler language may limit abstract thinking: language shaping thought.","658":"Elaborated and restricted codes are Bernstein's idea.","659":"Language is a structured system of communication, unique to humans.","660":"Communication is passing information between people or animals.","661":"Verbal = using words.","662":"Non-verbal = without words: eye contact, posture, personal space.","663":"Paralinguistics = how you say it: tone, pitch, volume and speed.","664":"Animals communicate to survive, reproduce, defend territory and find food.","665":"Different alarm calls help monkeys escape different predators: survival.","666":"A peacock's tail attracts a mate: reproduction.","667":"Scent marks and birdsong claim an area: territory.","668":"The waggle dance and pheromone trails lead others to food.","669":"Scent marking claims territory with smell.","670":"Pheromones are chemical signals between animals of the same species.","671":"A peacock's tail is visual; a lion's roar or birdsong is sound.","673":"Displacement means talking about things not here and now, like the past or future.","674":"A closed system has a fixed set of signals: typical of animals.","675":"Creativity means making new messages: unique to humans.","676":"Single-channel means one signal type at a time: typical of animals.","677":"Karl von Frisch studied bee dances.","678":"An ethologist studies animals in their natural habitat.","679":"The round dance means food is close (under about 100 m).","680":"The waggle dance means food is further away; its direction and speed give the location.","681":"Von Frisch moved the food closer or further away from the hive.","682":"Thousands of observations of bees in a glass hive, over about 20 years.","683":"Von Frisch watched the bees for about 20 years.","684":"Bees dance rather than use words, so it's non-verbal.","685":"A glass hive, sugar water and marked bees aren't natural.","686":"Weather and other factors could affect the dances.","687":"Von Frisch's work was a breakthrough in animal communication, and won a Nobel Prize.","688":"Eye contact regulates conversation, signals attraction and expresses emotion.","689":"Looking away and back signals whose turn it is to speak.","690":"Speakers looked away as they finished, signalling turn-taking.","691":"Without eye contact, conversation became clumsy.","692":"Faces making eye contact were rated as more attractive.","693":"Direct gaze intensified joy and anger; averted gaze intensified fear and sadness.","694":"Eye contact matters in interviews and classrooms.","695":"Body language is gestures and postures that show feelings.","696":"Posture is how you hold your body.","697":"An open posture shows friendliness and openness.","698":"A closed posture can signal defensiveness.","699":"Postural echo is copying someone's posture, which shows rapport.","700":"Open posture and mirroring build rapport.","701":"A light touch makes people feel more positive.","702":"Fisher's touch study took place in a university library.","703":"Students lightly touched by the librarian rated the library more positively.","704":"No consent, but natural behaviour, so high validity.","705":"Personal space is your comfortable bubble.","706":"Culture, gender and status affect personal space.","707":"Contact cultures stand closer; non-contact cultures keep more distance.","708":"Contact cultures include Italy, Spain, Arab countries and Latin America.","709":"Arab men stood closer: a cultural difference.","710":"Matching Arab-style personal space made English men more liked.","711":"Higher status means more space is kept.","712":"Men keep more distance from men than women do from women.","713":"Women disliked strangers sitting beside them more than opposite.","714":"Personality, familiarity, setting and age can also affect personal space.","715":"Darwin proposed the evolutionary theory of non-verbal behaviour.","716":"Adaptive behaviour helps survival or reproduction, so it gets passed on.","717":"A serviceable habit was once useful for survival and is now used to express emotion.","718":"These behaviours are still in our genes.","719":"Wide eyes help an animal see danger and escape routes.","720":"Humans widen their eyes to show fear or surprise.","721":"Wrinkling the nose once blocked bad smells; now it shows disgust.","722":"Darwin said non-verbal behaviour evolved, so it's innate.","723":"Innate = present from birth.","724":"Neonate research studies newborns to see what's innate.","725":"Social releasers are baby behaviours that trigger care.","726":"Babies do these from birth, so they're innate.","727":"Social releasers are John Bowlby's idea.","728":"Sensory deprived = missing a sense, e.g. born blind.","729":"Blind children make the same expressions without seeing them, so the expressions are innate.","730":"Blind-from-birth children still showed normal expressions.","731":"Cultural differences suggest non-verbal communication is learned.","732":"Gestures mean different things in different places, so they're learned.","733":"Social learning theory: we learn by watching and copying others.","734":"Emoticon = emotion + icon.","735":"Yuki compared the USA and Japan.","736":"Yuki tested whether cultures read emotion from eyes or mouths.","737":"Japanese participants read emotion from the eyes.","738":"Americans focused on the mouth.","739":"Different people in each group (two cultures): independent groups.","740":"Real faces are more realistic than emoticons.","741":"Culture changes how we read faces, so non-verbal communication is partly learned.","742":"Emoticons are artificial, and only two emotions were tested.","743":"Rating scales are subjective.","744":"The named Language studies are von Frisch (bees) and Yuki (emoticons).","1034":"Neonates are babies under a month old.","1035":"Survival = staying alive.","1036":"Territory is an area defended against others.","1037":"Touch is physical contact, a form of non-verbal communication.","1177":"The child's schema isn't developed yet, so the words don't fit, according to Piaget.","1178":"No concept means no understanding of the word: thought first (Piaget).","1179":"Separate words help them notice differences: the weak version of Sapir-Whorf.","1180":"They think about direction without the word, which challenges the strong version.","1181":"Studies show thought without language, which supports Piaget.","1182":"Schemas can't be directly measured.","1183":"Colour studies show language affects memory and perception.","1184":"People can think about things they have no words for.","1185":"Language helps thinking but doesn't fully limit it.","1186":"Piaget: thought first. Sapir-Whorf: language first.","1187":"New words can help new thinking, and help us understand other cultures.","1188":"Translation problems and cultural misunderstanding make the research hard.","1189":"Experience of snow comes first, then the words: Piaget.","1190":"Singing at the edge of its area defends territory.","1191":"An alarm call keeps the group alive: survival.","1192":"Displaying feathers attracts a mate: reproduction.","1193":"A scent trail leads to food.","1194":"Rubbing scent marks territory.","1195":"An open system has unlimited new messages.","1196":"New slang or poems show creativity.","1197":"Words, tone and face together are multi-channel.","1198":"Humans can talk about the past and future (displacement); animals stick to the here and now.","1199":"A directional hypothesis predicts far food → more waggle dances.","1200":"The IV was food distance.","1201":"The DV was which dance the bees performed.","1202":"In the natural environment, the bees behaved naturally.","1203":"Bees aren't humans, so the findings may not apply to us.","1204":"Marking or handling bees raises ethical questions.","1205":"Thousands of consistent observations, replicated by others, make it reliable.","1206":"Looking away and back regulates conversation.","1207":"Catching each other's eye and smiling signals attraction.","1208":"Downcast, tearful eyes express emotion.","1209":"Eye contact research has real uses, e.g. in interviews and teaching.","1210":"Photos and labs aren't like real conversations.","1211":"In some cultures direct eye contact is rude.","1212":"Sitting the same way is postural echo.","1213":"Folded arms suggest a closed posture.","1214":"Mirroring builds rapport.","1215":"A real setting means natural behaviour, so high ecological validity.","1216":"Only women were studied, so it may not apply to men.","1217":"How often people touch varies by culture.","1218":"Italy is a contact culture, so people stand closer.","1219":"More distance from the head shows status.","1220":"Women side by side and men opposite reflects gender.","1221":"In real life, culture, gender and status combine.","1222":"Personal space research can help design offices, classrooms and transport.","1223":"Similar expressions everywhere, even in blind children, support Darwin.","1224":"Darwin can't explain cultural differences in gestures.","1225":"We can't go back and watch behaviours evolve.","1226":"Blind children couldn't have learned expressions by seeing them.","1227":"A small sample, and some may have learned through touch or sound.","1228":"Babies smile from birth, which supports the innate view.","1229":"Gestures that vary by culture must be learned.","1230":"Probably both: some expressions are innate, but culture shapes their use.","1231":"The IV was culture and the emoticon's eye and mouth combination.","1232":"The DV was ratings of how happy or sad the faces looked.","1233":"A directional hypothesis predicts Japanese focus on eyes, Americans on mouths.","1234":"Standardised emoticons and lab control make it repeatable.","1235":"Yuki's findings can help design emojis for international audiences.","1236":"Students from two countries aren't representative of everyone.","1237":"Emojis are non-verbal cues in texts.","1238":"Westerners read mouths, so :-) uses the mouth.","1239":"Japanese read eyes, so (^_^) uses the eyes.","1465":"Students from two cultures rated six emoticons from very sad to very happy.","1466":"Pullum said the Inuit 'many words for snow' claim was exaggerated.","1467":"Boas made the claim about Inuit snow words.","1468":"A drawing's label changed how people remembered it.","1469":"Simple drawings aren't like real events.","1470":"Bees also use sound and vibration, not just dance.","1471":"Universal facial expressions suggest they evolved.","1472":"The 'OK' sign and pointing vary by culture.","1473":"Experience of snow came first, then the words: Piaget.","1":"Encoding is the first step: information has to be turned into a code (sound, image or meaning) before it can be stored.","2":"Think of the three ways in: what it looks like (visual), sounds like (acoustic) and means (semantic).","3":"Visual = vision: you store a mental picture, like remembering what a page of notes looked like.","6":"Storage is the middle step of memory: encode it, store it, then retrieve it later.","7":"Retrieval = getting it back out, like retrieving a file from a folder.","8":"Recall is a blank page (no help); recognition is a multiple-choice question (you just spot it).","9":"Free = any order you like, with no clues. Murdock's participants used free recall.","10":"A cue is a hint, so cued recall gives you a clue, like the first letter of a word.","14":"Episodic = episodes of YOUR life. Semantic = facts anyone could know.","15":"Episodic (events), semantic (facts) and procedural (skills, like riding a bike).","18":"Non-declarative means you can't easily put it into words: you just do it, like riding a bike.","19":"You can declare (say out loud) events and facts, so episodic and semantic are declarative.","20":"Episodic sits on the right of the pre-frontal area, semantic on the left, and skills in the motor cortex, which controls movement.","21":"The multi-store model is Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968). Bartlett and Murdock did memory studies, not this model.","22":"Information flows sensory register → STM → LTM, like boxes in a line.","23":"Your senses take in a huge amount at once, so capacity is very large, but most of it is lost almost instantly.","24":"The sensory register only holds information for a blink: under half a second unless you pay attention.","25":"Modality specific = each sense has its own store, so sights are held visually and sounds acoustically.","29":"LTM has no known limit: you never 'fill up' your memory.","30":"LTM can last a lifetime, as Bahrick's yearbook study showed.","32":"Attention is the gate: you only notice and keep what you pay attention to.","33":"Rehearsing over and over moves it from STM into LTM.","34":"Maintenance rehearsal keeps something in STM, like repeating a phone number until you dial it.","35":"Prolonged rehearsal goes further than maintenance: enough repetition moves it into LTM.","36":"Without rehearsal, STM items fade (decay) or get pushed out by new ones (displacement).","37":"To use a long-term memory, it's retrieved back into STM, where you're aware of it.","38":"George Miller found STM holds about 7 ± 2 items: his 'magic number seven'.","39":"A digit span test keeps adding digits until you can't repeat them back, which shows your STM capacity.","41":"Bahrick used high school yearbooks to test long-term memory of classmates' names and faces.","42":"Even after 48 years, recognition was 70–80%, so LTM can last for decades.","43":"Memories lasting nearly 50 years support the MSM's claim that LTM duration is unlimited.","44":"Clive Wearing lost the ability to make new long-term memories but can still play piano (procedural memory).","46":"Research like Murdock's serial position curve fits the MSM's idea of separate STM and LTM stores.","47":"The MSM has one LTM box, but Clive Wearing lost some memories and kept others, so LTM must have parts.","48":"Reductionist = oversimplified: real memory is more complex than three boxes and rehearsal.","49":"Murdock wanted to see if a word's position in a list affects whether it's remembered.","50":"Lists of 10–40 words, then free recall in any order: that's how Murdock found the serial position curve.","51":"The IV is what Murdock changed: where a word came in the list.","52":"The DV is what was measured: how many words were recalled.","53":"Murdock's study was a laboratory experiment: controlled conditions, with word lists presented the same way.","54":"The graph of recall by position is a U shape, called the serial position curve.","55":"Start words were rehearsed into LTM (primacy) and end words were still in STM (recency).","60":"Middle words get neither benefit: not rehearsed enough for LTM, and pushed out of STM by later words.","61":"Primacy (LTM) and recency (STM) effects are evidence that the two stores are separate.","62":"One-tailed means it predicts a direction: start and end words recalled MORE than middle words.","63":"Murdock wanted to generalise to everyone with a memory, not just students.","64":"Standardised = the same for everyone, e.g. each word shown for the same length of time.","65":"Learning random word lists isn't something we do in real life, so it lacks mundane realism.","68":"Because procedures were standardised, others can repeat the study exactly, so it's reliable.","69":"Primacy and recency mean the start and end of a session stick best, so put key facts there.","70":"Bartlett (1932) proposed reconstructive memory, tested with the War of the Ghosts.","71":"Recall is rebuilding, not replaying: we fill gaps with what we expect.","72":"Memory isn't a recording that plays back exactly; it's rebuilt each time we recall.","73":"Confabulation = filling memory gaps with made-up details without realising.","74":"Schemas are our expectations, so we fill gaps with what usually happens.","75":"We try to make a memory make sense by fitting it into what we already know.","76":"Levelling flattens the story: details are dropped and it gets shorter.","77":"Sharpening makes some details stand out more, often exaggerated.","78":"Rationalisation changes odd details so they make sense in our own culture.","79":"A schema is a mental 'packet' of knowledge, like what usually happens at a birthday party.","82":"Bartlett's English participants turned unfamiliar details into familiar ones, like canoe → boat.","83":"Bartlett's story was a Native American folk tale called 'The War of the Ghosts'.","84":"Bartlett tested reconstructive memory: the story changed every time it was recalled.","85":"English students retold an unfamiliar Native American story after different gaps of time.","86":"Bartlett used 20 English students from Cambridge University: a small, unrepresentative sample.","87":"Repeated reproduction = the same person retells the story again and again over time.","88":"Serial reproduction = passed from person to person, like Chinese whispers.","89":"Bartlett measured how the story changed, i.e. how accurate each retelling was.","90":"The story got shorter (levelling) and was changed to fit English culture (rationalisation).","91":"Leaving things out and making it shorter is levelling.","92":"Sharpening means some details are exaggerated or made more dramatic.","93":"Changing seals to fishing and canoes to boats fitted English schemas: that's rationalisation.","94":"Counting words is quantitative (a number); describing changes is qualitative.","95":"Because the story changed to fit their culture, Bartlett concluded memory is reconstructive.","96":"Two-tailed predicts a difference but not which group does better.","97":"Bartlett didn't control the gaps between retellings, so time could affect results.","98":"Ethnocentric = judging from one culture's view; Bartlett only used English students.","99":"Without standardised instructions or timings, the study can't be repeated in exactly the same way.","100":"20 Cambridge students aren't typical of everyone, so the findings may not generalise.","101":"Reconstructive memory helps explain why eyewitnesses get details wrong.","102":"If memories are rebuilt, any one witness could be wrong, so collect several accounts.","103":"Forgetting means you can't get the memory back out, even though it was once stored.","104":"Interference (other info gets in the way), context (place) and false memories (things that never happened).","109":"McGeoch and McDonald tested whether learning a second list interferes with the first.","110":"Participants learned one list perfectly, then a second, different list, before recalling the first.","111":"Synonyms are the most similar to the original words, so they interfere the most.","112":"The more similar the two activities, the more they interfere.","113":"Interference ignores other causes of forgetting, such as context, so it's reductionist.","115":"Godden and Baddeley tested whether matching the learning and recall places helps memory.","116":"Divers learned on land or underwater, then recalled in the same or a different place.","117":"The IV was whether the learning and recall environments matched.","118":"Recall was best when learning and recall happened in the same place, so context acts as a cue.","119":"Matching environments give the same cues, which trigger the memory.","121":"Everyone had the same word lists and timings, so it can be repeated: reliable.","122":"Learning lists underwater isn't an everyday task, so it lacks mundane realism.","123":"Being back in the same place gives the same cues, helping recall.","124":"Encoding leaves a memory trace, a physical change in the brain.","125":"A cue is a trigger, like a smell or place, that brings a memory back.","126":"A false memory feels real but never actually happened.","127":"'Lost in the mall' tested whether a false childhood memory could be implanted.","128":"Three real childhood stories and one false one (lost in a mall), all from relatives.","129":"A quarter of participants 'remembered' being lost in the mall, though it never happened.","130":"If imagining an event can create a memory, memory can't always be trusted.","131":"Suggestion is when someone guides your thoughts, e.g. with a leading question.","132":"Repressed memories are blocked out because they are too upsetting to face.","133":"Leading questions or suggestions can plant details that never happened.","134":"Leading questions can create false memories, so police should ask open questions.","135":"Participants were deceived about a childhood event, which could be upsetting.","136":"Being lost in a mall isn't traumatic, so it may not show how real false memories form.","137":"The two named Memory studies are Murdock (serial position) and Bartlett (War of the Ghosts).","138":"A theory explains behaviour; a study tests whether the explanation is right.","140":"GRAVE = Generalisability, Reliability, Application, Validity, Ethics.","141":"TDEE = Term, Define, Evidence, Explain: the four parts of an evaluation paragraph.","1013":"Capacity = how much fits, like the capacity of a bottle.","1014":"Coding (encoding) changes information into a form that can be stored.","1015":"Duration = how long a memory lasts.","1016":"The long-term store holds lots of information for a long time.","1017":"Recognition is spotting something you've seen before.","1018":"Reconstructive memory fills gaps so the memory makes sense, rather than replaying it exactly.","1382":"It's about her own experience on a particular day, so it's episodic.","1383":"It's a general fact anyone can know, so it's semantic.","1384":"It's a skill done without thinking, so it's procedural.","1385":"Repeating something to keep it in STM is maintenance rehearsal.","1386":"Chunking groups items together so more fit in STM.","1387":"Retro = backwards: the new (Spanish) interferes with the old (French).","1388":"Revising in the exam room gives the same context cues at recall.","1389":"The detail came from the officer's suggestion, not what she saw: a false memory.","1390":"Leaving out = levelling; adding exciting details = sharpening.","1391":"First items are in LTM (primacy) and last items are still in STM (recency).","1392":"A story is closer to real-life memory than word lists, so it has more mundane realism.","1393":"Participants just read and retold a story, so there was little risk of harm.","1394":"Standardised word lists in a lab mean high control, so the study can be repeated.","1395":"Only 12 participants and artificial word lists limit generalisation and realism.","1396":"The IV is what changed: the type of second list.","1397":"The DV is what was measured: how well the first list was recalled.","1398":"Each diver did all four conditions, so it was repeated measures.","1399":"They used divers from a diving club who agreed to take part.","1400":"The DV was the number of words each diver recalled.","1401":"Participants volunteered, recruited with help from their relatives.","1402":"Relatives confirmed which events really happened, so the researchers knew which memory was false.","1403":"Pay attention to get it into STM, then rehearse it to move it into LTM.","1404":"Similar topics interfere most, so space them out.","1405":"Land vs underwater is an extreme contrast; most everyday places are much more alike.","1406":"Distinctive or emotional details are often remembered accurately, which the theory struggles to explain.","1407":"He lost one type (episodic) but kept another (procedural), so they must be separate.","1408":"A null hypothesis predicts no difference.","1409":"Cold, deep water could harm the divers, so protection from harm matters.","1442":"Murdock's participants were psychology students.","1443":"Psychology students may know about memory and guess the aim, so the sample is biased.","1444":"Random words controlled for meaning, so position is likely to explain the results.","1445":"Amnesiacs lose primacy (LTM) but keep recency (STM), so the stores must be separate.","1446":"An unfamiliar folk tale isn't like remembering everyday events.","1447":"Baddeley compared words that sound alike with words that mean alike, tested immediately or after 20 minutes.","1448":"Sound-alike words confused STM; meaning-alike words confused LTM.","1449":"STM codes mainly by sound, LTM mainly by meaning, so they are different stores.","1450":"Random word lists aren't like real-life memory, so the evidence lacks mundane realism.","1451":"Damage to one store but not the other suggests STM and LTM are separate.","1452":"Tulving found semantic memories lit up the back of the brain and episodic the front.","1453":"Episodic and semantic memories often blend together, so they may not be fully separate.","1454":"Use TDEE when your point has a key term to define, and PEE when it doesn't.","183":"Sensation is the raw input: light, sound or pressure hitting your sense receptors.","184":"Perception is the brain making sense of what the senses pick up, using past experience.","185":"Sensing = detecting; perceiving = interpreting.","186":"Each sense has its own name: visual, auditory, olfactory (smell), gustatory (taste) and tactile (touch).","187":"Past experience and knowledge let the brain interpret what we sense.","188":"An ambiguous figure can 'flip' between two pictures, like a duck and a rabbit.","189":"Visual cues are clues in what we see that tell us about distance, movement and position.","190":"Depth cues are clues that tell us how far away things are.","193":"Binocular = both eyes: retinal disparity and convergence need two eyes.","195":"The closer something is, the more your eyes turn inwards, and the brain feels that strain.","196":"Convergence needs both eyes turning inwards, so it's binocular.","199":"Each eye sees a slightly different picture, so retinal disparity needs two eyes: binocular.","200":"These all work with one eye, which is why artists can use them in flat paintings.","205":"Things far away look smoother because you can't see their detail.","207":"Constancies stop the world seeming to change shape, size or colour as we move.","209":"A door seen at an angle is still perceived as a rectangle.","210":"A white shirt looks white in daylight and under orange street lights.","211":"An illusion happens when what we perceive doesn't match what's really there.","212":"Misread depth (Müller-Lyer), two possible images (ambiguity), or seeing something that isn't there (fiction).","216":"Rubin's vase flips between a vase and two faces, so it's ambiguous.","217":"There's no triangle drawn, but we 'see' one: that's fiction.","219":"Ambiguous = two possible meanings, like the duck-rabbit.","220":"Gregory = constructivist: we construct perception. Gibson = direct.","221":"Gregory says we build perception from sensory data plus what we already know: top-down.","224":"Inference = a conclusion drawn from what we already know.","225":"A perceptual hypothesis is our best guess about what we're seeing.","226":"Gregory says perception is learned from experience: nurture.","227":"More experience means better guesses, so perception improves with age.","228":"Illusions trick our learned guesses, showing perception relies on experience.","229":"Different cultures have different experiences, so perceptions that differ must be learned.","230":"Illusions are designed to trick us; real life gives us much more information.","231":"If babies can perceive depth very early, some perception must be innate, not learned.","232":"Gibson = direct: we perceive straight from the environment. Gregory = constructivist.","233":"Gibson says the senses give all the information we need, with no guessing required: bottom-up.","236":"The optic array is all the light reaching our eyes, carrying everything needed to perceive.","237":"Affordances are what something lets you do: a chair affords sitting, a cup affords drinking.","239":"Gibson says we're born able to perceive directly: nature.","240":"The visual cliff was Gibson and Walk's study.","241":"A glass table looked like a drop, and babies wouldn't crawl over it even when their mothers called.","242":"Refusing the 'drop' early in life suggests depth perception is innate.","243":"If perception were direct and accurate, illusions shouldn't fool us.","244":"By 6 months, babies have had time to learn about depth.","245":"Perceptual set is a readiness to notice some things and ignore others.","246":"Culture, emotion, expectation and motivation all affect perceptual set.","247":"Culture means the shared customs and beliefs of a group of people.","248":"Experience of pictures and surroundings varies between cultures, so perception is learned differently.","249":"Hudson (1960) studied culture and perception, using pictures of a hunter.","250":"Hudson tested schooled and unschooled people in South Africa.","251":"Depth cues show the hunter aiming at the antelope, not the elephant.","252":"Schooling (experience of pictures) helped people read the depth cues.","253":"Without experience of 2D pictures, they didn't use the depth cues, so the elephant looked closer.","254":"Books and pictures had taught them to read depth cues in 2D images.","255":"Reading depth in pictures depends on culture and experience, so it's learned.","256":"Misunderstood questions, an artificial task and an old study all weaken it.","257":"Emotion steers attention: we're slower to notice upsetting things.","258":"Perceptual defence: the brain delays noticing upsetting things.","259":"Unpleasant things take longer to perceive because of perceptual defence.","260":"McGinnies (1949) used taboo (rude) words to study emotion.","261":"Participants were shown neutral and taboo words while GSR measured emotional arousal.","262":"Taboo words were recognised more slowly and caused more arousal.","263":"GSR measures tiny changes in how well skin conducts electricity when we're aroused.","264":"We see what we expect to see, based on past experience.","265":"Bruner and Minturn studied expectation, created by context (letters vs numbers).","266":"Bruner and Minturn's study was in 1955.","267":"It was carried out in a lab at Harvard University.","268":"A controlled lab experiment, with the IV (context) changed by the researchers.","270":"Two groups saw letters or numbers first, then the same ambiguous figure.","271":"The figure could be read as a B or as 13.","272":"Letters first → saw B; numbers first → saw 13. Context created the expectation.","273":"The context set up an expectation, which changed what people perceived.","274":"Flashing figures designed to trick people isn't an everyday task.","275":"If they spotted the trick, participants might guess the aim and change their answers.","276":"We notice what we need: hungry people notice food.","277":"Gilchrist and Nesberg (1952) studied motivation (hunger).","278":"They were shown pictures of meals, because the study was about hunger.","279":"First they saw a food picture at a set brightness, to compare against later.","280":"They adjusted the brightness to match the original, which showed how bright the food looked to them.","281":"The IV was how hungry participants were (hours without food).","282":"The hungrier people were, the brighter food looked to them.","283":"Needs (hunger) shape what we notice, so motivation affects perceptual set.","285":"Going 20 hours without food and rating pictures isn't normal life.","286":"Poorer children saw coins as bigger, because they valued them more.","287":"The two named Perception studies are Bruner and Minturn (expectation) and Gilchrist and Nesberg (motivation).","1020":"Inference = filling in what you see using what you already know.","1021":"Monocular cues work with just one eye.","1061":"He can still judge depth with one eye shut, so he's using monocular cues.","1062":"Eye strain from looking at something close is convergence.","1063":"Parallel lines meeting in the distance is linear perspective.","1064":"One object blocking another is occlusion: the blocker is closer.","1065":"Higher up in the picture = further away: height in plane.","1066":"Smaller looks further away: relative size.","1067":"Detail fading with distance is texture gradient.","1068":"Near things move faster across your view than far ones: motion parallax.","1069":"Still seeing a rectangle at an angle is shape constancy.","1070":"Not thinking they're shrinking is size constancy.","1071":"White still looks white under different light: colour constancy.","1072":"Two possible images is ambiguity.","1073":"Seeing a shape that isn't drawn is fiction.","1074":"The fins act like depth cues, so we misjudge the line lengths.","1075":"Using past experience to guess is top-down processing.","1076":"Newborns haven't had time to learn, so this supports innate (direct) perception.","1077":"Experience shapes perception, so this supports Gregory.","1078":"Hunger (a need) directs attention: motivation.","1079":"Anxiety makes negative things stand out: emotion.","1080":"Reading words made her expect letters: expectation.","1081":"Less experience of 2D drawings in their culture: culture.","1082":"The fins look like corners of buildings, so one line seems further away and is perceived as longer.","1083":"Gregory's guessing can go wrong (illusions); Gibson's direct perception shouldn't.","1084":"Real-life perception is fast, and the visual cliff suggests innate abilities.","1085":"Gibson can't explain illusions or cultural differences.","1086":"Gregory explains illusions (wrong guesses) and culture (different experience).","1087":"Flat illusions aren't like rich real-world scenes.","1088":"Gregory = nurture (learned); Gibson = nature (innate).","1089":"The same picture and questions for everyone means it can be repeated: reliable.","1090":"Confusion about questions or drawings could explain the answers, not perception.","1091":"Lots has changed since 1960, so results may not apply today.","1092":"Looking at pictures carries little risk of harm.","1093":"GSR is an objective physical measure, so it's less open to bias.","1094":"They may have seen the taboo words but been embarrassed to say them aloud.","1095":"16 students is a small, unrepresentative sample.","1096":"Everyone saw both types of word, so it was repeated measures.","1097":"The same sequences and timings for everyone means it can be repeated.","1098":"24 university students isn't a representative sample.","1099":"The IV was what people saw first: letters or numbers.","1100":"The DV was whether they saw a B or a 13.","1101":"Just viewing figures caused no harm or distress.","1102":"A directional hypothesis predicts letters first → more likely to see B.","1103":"The DV was how bright they rated the food pictures.","1104":"Standard procedures and brightness ratings make it repeatable.","1105":"26 student volunteers is small and not representative.","1106":"Going 20 hours without food could be uncomfortable, even with consent.","1107":"A directional hypothesis predicts that hungry participants rate food as brighter.","1108":"Hungry people notice food more, so place adverts where hungry people are.","1109":"Perceptual set explains what we notice, which is useful for adverts, road signs and witnesses.","1110":"Flat images on screens aren't like real 3D scenes.","1111":"Ambiguous figures can be seen two ways: duck-rabbit, vase/faces, B/13.","1112":"We assume rooms are rectangular, so the size difference fools us.","1113":"The converging lines act as depth cues, so we apply size constancy and misjudge size.","1114":"Gregory: sensing and perceiving are different steps. Gibson: they're the same.","1115":"Babies avoided the 'cliff', which suggests early depth perception.","1116":"6-month-olds already have experience, so it might be learned.","1117":"Each person saw only one context, so they couldn't guess the aim from doing both.","1118":"Different needs and experiences make people notice different things.","1119":"Without practice with pictures, people may not read depth cues in drawings.","1120":"Ambiguous figures are rare in real life, so findings may not apply.","1455":"Optic flow is the way the scene streams past as you move.","1456":"Closer things look brighter and richer in colour, further things duller.","1457":"Babies preferring a real face suggests face perception is innate.","1458":"Studies of babies tell us little about adult perception.","1459":"Gibson ignores experience (nurture), which oversimplifies perception.","1460":"Gregory ignores innate abilities (nature), which oversimplifies perception.","1461":"Different people in each group could differ in ways that affect results.","1462":"26 student volunteers in two groups: hungry and control.","1463":"The longer without food, the more people saw food in pictures.","1464":"SPACER evaluates theories; GRAVE evaluates studies.","869":"Mental health problems affect thinking, feeling or behaviour enough to make coping harder.","870":"Mental health is emotional and psychological wellbeing.","871":"Good mental health means coping, feeling good about yourself and being independent.","872":"Poor mental health shows as low mood, worry, isolation and struggling to cope.","873":"Serious mental illnesses include schizophrenia, bipolar disorder and severe depression.","874":"Ideal mental health is Jahoda's classification.","875":"Jahoda's six: self-attitudes, growth, integration, autonomy, reality perception and mastery of the environment.","876":"Autonomy = independence.","877":"Self-actualisation = reaching your potential.","878":"Jahoda describes what good mental health looks like, not just symptoms.","879":"Few people meet every criterion, and they reflect Western ideas.","880":"About 1 in 6 adults in England have a common mental health problem.","881":"Less stigma, better diagnosis and the stresses of modern life all add to the rise.","882":"Treatment rose from 24% to 37% of adults.","883":"Low income, city living and older age are linked to higher rates.","884":"Modern living means technology and fast-paced life.","885":"Stigma is shame or disapproval.","886":"The same experience can be seen as illness or as spiritual, depending on culture.","887":"Individualistic = focused on the individual, e.g. the UK.","888":"More cases are reported in individualistic cultures.","889":"Individual effects hit the person: relationships, coping and physical health.","890":"Social effects hit society: care costs, crime and the economy.","891":"Stress hormones weaken the immune system.","892":"Social care is support services for people in need.","893":"The McCrone report estimated about £22 billion a year.","894":"Walsh found a higher risk of violence linked to serious mental illness.","895":"Other factors may explain the link, and most violent crime isn't by people with mental illness.","896":"Isolation means being cut off from others.","897":"Sadness passes; depression lasts.","898":"Unipolar = mood goes one way: down.","899":"Bipolar = two poles: lows and highs.","900":"Mania is an extreme high: lots of energy, little sleep, risk-taking.","901":"Low mood, low energy and loss of interest are core symptoms.","902":"Behavioural = actions, emotional = feelings, cognitive = thoughts.","903":"Doctors diagnose using the ICD (or DSM).","904":"The ICD is produced by the World Health Organization.","905":"Symptoms must last at least two weeks.","906":"The ICD lists mood, interest, energy and thinking symptoms.","907":"The ICD rates depression as mild, moderate or severe by number and severity of symptoms.","908":"Physical illnesses can mimic depression, so they must be ruled out.","910":"Biological: low serotonin.","911":"Serotonin regulates mood, sleep and appetite.","912":"Low serotonin can come from genes or a diet low in tryptophan.","913":"Tryptophan is the building block of serotonin.","914":"Exercise, diet and sunlight can boost serotonin.","915":"People with depression had lower serotonin.","916":"Exercise raises serotonin.","917":"Low serotonin might be an effect, not a cause, and the explanation ignores thoughts.","918":"Antidepressants are drugs that treat depression.","919":"SSRI = selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.","921":"Blocking reuptake keeps more serotonin in the synapse.","922":"Vesicles store neurotransmitters.","923":"SSRIs can cause unpleasant side effects.","925":"SSRIs are cheap, quick and easy compared with therapy.","926":"Cognitive: negative thinking causes depression.","928":"The negative triad: negative thoughts about the self, the world and the future.","930":"Childhood criticism or rejection can build a negative self-schema.","931":"Seeing everything negatively lowers mood.","932":"Attributions are how we explain why things happen.","933":"Negative attributional style is Seligman's idea.","934":"Internal = 'it's me'; external = 'it's something else'.","935":"Stable = permanent; unstable = temporary.","936":"Global = it affects everything in life.","937":"Blaming yourself, forever, for everything leads to depression.","938":"Beck's explanation led to CBT, which works.","939":"It can seem to blame the person, and the direction of cause is unclear.","940":"CBT = cognitive behavioural therapy.","941":"CBT changes irrational thoughts and behaviours.","942":"In CBT, the person is called the client.","943":"Catastrophising: one small thing means disaster.","944":"Disputing means challenging irrational thoughts with evidence.","945":"A thought diary records feelings, the thoughts behind them and a rational response.","946":"Doing pleasant activities lifts mood.","947":"Skills learned in CBT can be reused if symptoms return.","948":"CBT treats thoughts and behaviour, not just chemistry.","949":"CBT needs time and effort, so some people drop out.","950":"Holistic = looking at the whole person.","951":"Wiles' study was the CoBalT trial.","952":"Wiles tested whether adding CBT helps when drugs alone haven't.","953":"Treatment-resistant = drugs alone haven't worked.","954":"Wiles had 469 participants: a large sample.","955":"The two conditions were drugs only or drugs plus CBT.","956":"Participants had 12–18 sessions of CBT.","957":"The BDI is a 21-question self-report depression score.","958":"Depression was measured at the start, at 6 months and at 12 months.","959":"About 90% stayed for the full year.","960":"With CBT added, 46.1% improved by more than half.","961":"Drugs alone: only 21.6% improved by more than half.","962":"CBT plus drugs worked better than drugs alone.","963":"Self-report can be exaggerated or played down.","964":"A large sample and random allocation make it strong.","965":"Addiction: being unable to stop.","966":"The ICD lists cravings, loss of control, persisting despite harm, tolerance and withdrawal.","967":"Three or more characteristics together in the past year.","973":"Using a medicine for its purpose but not as prescribed is misuse.","974":"Genetic vulnerability: inherited risk.","975":"Hereditary = passed on through genes.","976":"Diathesis (genes) + stress (environment) = disorder.","977":"Genes load the gun; the environment pulls the trigger.","978":"MZ = from one egg, identical, sharing 100% of their genes.","979":"DZ = from two eggs, non-identical, sharing about 50% of their genes.","982":"Kaij's study took place in Sweden.","983":"Kaij used the temperance board register.","984":"174 pairs of male twins.","985":"Identical twins: 54.2% had a co-twin also registered.","986":"Non-identical twins: 28.3%.","988":"Male Swedish twins only, from a biased register.","989":"Kendler found the same pattern: higher in MZ than DZ twins.","990":"A peer is someone like you in age or group.","991":"Peer influence: people like us shaping what we do.","992":"Peer influence is strongest in adolescence.","993":"Copying peers who use substances.","994":"Vicarious reinforcement: seeing someone else rewarded makes you copy them.","995":"If peers think it's normal, we copy it.","996":"Almost every study linked peers to smoking.","997":"Some people become addicted without peers who use.","998":"Classical conditioning is learning by association.","999":"Aversion therapy pairs the addiction with something unpleasant.","1001":"Antabuse makes alcohol cause sickness.","1002":"Rapid smoking means smoking until you feel sick.","1003":"The effect can fade, and many people drop out.","1004":"Extinction: the association fades when it's no longer paired.","1005":"Self-management: no professional leader.","1006":"A self-help group is people with the same problem supporting each other.","1007":"Alcoholics Anonymous and Narcotics Anonymous are self-help groups.","1008":"A 12-step programme gives 12 principles to follow.","1009":"Self-help groups are free, with support from people who understand.","1010":"They don't suit everyone and have no professional support.","1011":"The named Problems studies are Wiles and Kaij.","1012":"W = right to withdraw.","1054":"12-step programmes are self-help groups with 12 principles.","1055":"Coping means managing problems calmly.","1056":"The ICD is the WHO's list of symptoms used for diagnosis.","1057":"Positive engagement: being curious and motivated.","1058":"Sadness is a normal reaction to something upsetting.","1059":"Symptoms are signs of a condition.","1060":"Therapies are treatments.","1410":"Over 2 weeks is enough time, but a doctor checks the symptoms.","1411":"Highs and lows suggest bipolar disorder.","1412":"'Because I'm stupid' (internal), 'always' (stable), 'everything' (global).","1413":"Blaming the test (external), just this once (unstable).","1414":"Asking for evidence is disputing.","1415":"Planning enjoyable activities is behavioural activation.","1416":"Needing more to get the same effect is tolerance.","1417":"Feeling ill when stopping is withdrawal.","1418":"Carrying on despite serious harm.","1419":"Copying friends is peer influence.","1420":"Feeling sick when drinking is aversion therapy.","1421":"AA is a self-help group.","1422":"Research support and drug treatments back the biological explanation.","1423":"Higher concordance in identical twins suggests genes play a part.","1424":"Not 100% concordance, and twins share an environment.","1425":"Genes plus environment is more complete.","1426":"Positive peer role models can prevent addiction.","1427":"Aversion therapy can work fast on the behaviour itself.","1428":"Causing sickness on purpose can be distressing.","1429":"Behaviour plus thoughts gives longer-lasting change.","1430":"The IV was the treatment: drugs only or drugs plus CBT.","1431":"The DV was depression score on the BDI.","1432":"469 UK adults recruited from GP practices.","1433":"Random allocation controlled participant variables.","1434":"The IV was twin type: MZ or DZ.","1435":"The DV was whether both twins had alcohol problems (concordance).","1436":"Kaij used interviews, questionnaires and register records.","1437":"People may play down their drinking.","1438":"More diagnoses may reflect more help-seeking, not more illness.","1439":"Headlines exaggerate the link.","1440":"Education and openness reduce stigma.","1441":"Drugs don't fix negative thinking or life problems.","144":"Psychology studies both what goes on inside (thoughts and feelings) and what we can see (behaviour), using scientific methods.","145":"The aim is the general purpose; the hypothesis is the specific, testable prediction.","146":"An aim names both variables: whether the DV is affected by the IV.","147":"I for 'I change it': the independent variable is the one the researcher manipulates.","148":"D for 'data': the dependent variable is what's measured.","150":"'Memory' is vague; 'number of words recalled out of 20' can be measured.","151":"A hypothesis is a precise prediction that can be tested.","153":"The alternative hypothesis says there IS an effect; the null says there isn't.","156":"A hypothesis states the DV and both conditions of the IV.","157":"A lab gives the most control: an artificial setting, with the IV changed by the researcher.","158":"Field = real-life setting, but the researcher still changes the IV.","159":"Natural = the IV happens by itself (e.g. age, gender); the researcher doesn't change it.","160":"Labs control extraneous variables best, so we can be more sure the IV caused the change.","165":"You can study things you couldn't ethically change, like the effects of a natural disaster.","166":"The IV isn't controlled or allocated, so other differences between groups could explain the results.","167":"Demand characteristics are clues to the aim that make participants act differently.","168":"Quantitative/qualitative is about numbers vs words; primary/secondary is about who collected it.","171":"Numbers are easy to compare, e.g. means and graphs.","172":"Numbers show what happened, not why.","173":"Words give rich detail about people's thoughts and feelings.","174":"Words must be interpreted, and different researchers may interpret them differently.","177":"Collecting it yourself means it fits your aim exactly.","178":"Someone else collected it for another purpose, so it may not fit your study or be up to date.","179":"Secondary data already exists, collected by someone else, e.g. government statistics.","288":"Clear, measurable variables let others repeat the study exactly.","289":"The alternative hypothesis predicts an effect: start and end words are recalled more.","290":"A null hypothesis predicts no difference.","291":"The target population is everyone you want the results to apply to.","292":"The sample is the smaller group who actually take part.","293":"A representative sample lets you generalise to the target population.","294":"Random = everyone has an equal chance, like names drawn from a hat.","295":"Chance can still produce a lopsided sample, e.g. mostly girls.","297":"People who happen to be there at the time may share characteristics, like all being shoppers.","299":"Systematic sampling takes every nth person, so only those positions can be picked.","301":"The researcher picks people likely to give the results they want.","302":"If the researcher's choice affects the results, it's not just the IV causing the change.","303":"Different people in each condition, the same people in both, or matched pairs.","307":"Doing a task twice can make you better (practice) or worse (fatigue/boredom).","309":"Finding pairs who match on key variables takes a lot of time and effort.","310":"Random allocation uses chance (e.g. a coin toss), so the researcher can't bias the groups.","311":"If everyone gets the same procedure, the study can be repeated and results compared.","315":"Zero correlation = no relationship: the dots are scattered with no pattern.","316":"Correlations are shown on a scattergram, with one co-variable on each axis.","318":"-1 is a perfect negative, 0 is none and +1 is a perfect positive correlation.","319":"A correlational hypothesis predicts a relationship between two co-variables.","320":"A third variable could cause both, e.g. hot weather increases ice cream sales and sunburn.","321":"Correlations can study variables that would be unethical to manipulate, like stress.","322":"A correlation shows a link, not cause and effect.","323":"An anomaly is a result far from the others.","325":"Covert = secret, so participants can't give informed consent.","326":"Knowing they're watched, people may act differently.","327":"Being part of the group can make the observer less objective.","328":"Over a long time, researchers may get attached and record subjectively.","458":"Overt = open (people know); covert = hidden (they don't).","459":"Naturalistic = in its normal setting, with no interference.","460":"Controlled = the researcher sets up the environment, e.g. a lab playroom.","461":"In a participant observation, the observer joins in with the group.","462":"In a non-participant observation, the observer watches from outside.","463":"Behavioural categories turn behaviour into clear things to count, e.g. 'hits', 'pushes'.","464":"Observations show what people do, not why, and observers may be biased.","465":"Observer bias: expectations affect what is recorded.","466":"If two observers record the same results, the observation is reliable.","467":"Percentages are numbers (quantitative), and you collected them yourself (primary).","470":"External = outside, i.e. whether results apply beyond the study.","473":"Standardised = the same for every participant.","474":"Random, opportunity, systematic and stratified are the four sampling methods.","475":"In random sampling, chance picks, not the researcher, so there's no selector bias.","476":"The researcher chooses where to start and how often to pick.","477":"The researcher decides which subgroups matter.","479":"Generalisability: can the results be applied to everyone in the target population?","480":"Independent groups = different people in each condition, so each does one condition.","481":"The BPS guidelines are the British Psychological Society's ethical rules.","482":"Ethical guidelines include consent, deception, right to withdraw, protection from harm, confidentiality and debriefing.","483":"Informed consent: people agree knowing what's involved.","484":"Deception: misleading participants or hiding the true aim.","485":"Right to withdraw: participants can leave at any time and take their data with them.","486":"Protection from harm: no physical or mental harm to participants.","487":"Confidentiality: participants' personal details are kept private.","488":"Debriefing: explaining the true aim afterwards and checking participants are OK.","489":"A case study is an in-depth study of one person or a small group, using several methods.","490":"Case studies give lots of detail, often over a long time.","491":"One unusual case may not apply to anyone else.","492":"Interviews ask questions directly, face to face or by phone.","493":"Structured = fixed questions in a fixed order.","494":"Unstructured = only the topic is fixed, and questions follow the conversation.","495":"Semi-structured = some set questions plus follow-ups.","496":"Semi-structured interviews are comparable (set questions) and detailed (follow-ups).","497":"Interviews take time, and people may answer to look good.","498":"A questionnaire is a set of written questions given to everyone.","501":"Closed questions have set answers to choose from, like a 1–5 scale.","502":"A Likert scale runs from 'strongly agree' to 'strongly disagree'.","503":"Questionnaires are quick and cheap for large numbers of people.","504":"People may give socially desirable answers rather than honest ones.","505":"Social desirability bias: answering to look good rather than honestly.","506":"A correlation shows a link, not that one variable causes the other.","1019":"Reductionist = reducing behaviour to simple parts, which may miss the bigger picture.","1023":"The BPS guidelines are the ethical code all psychologists in the UK follow.","1027":"An observation study involves watching and recording behaviour.","1028":"Stratified = in proportion, e.g. 60% girls in the school means 60% girls in the sample.","1029":"Systematic = every nth person on a list.","1030":"Ethical issues are about what's right for participants.","1043":"A normal distribution is a symmetrical bell curve, with mean, median and mode in the middle.","1290":"The IV is what's changed: music or no music.","1291":"The DV is what's measured: reaction time.","1292":"Anything other than music that could affect reaction time is an extraneous variable.","1293":"'Happiness' becomes measurable as a score out of 10.","1294":"'Aggression' becomes countable acts in a set time.","1295":"'Memory' becomes the number of words recalled out of 20.","1296":"Age can't be manipulated by the researcher, so it's a natural experiment.","1297":"A real classroom (natural setting) but the researcher changes the music: a field experiment.","1298":"An artificial lab with the IV changed by the researcher: a laboratory experiment.","1301":"Pairs are matched on IQ and split between conditions: matched pairs.","1306":"Matching reduces participant variables, and no one does both conditions.","1307":"You can never match people perfectly, and finding pairs takes time.","1309":"Every 10th name is a fixed interval: systematic sampling.","1310":"Whoever happens to be passing: opportunity sampling.","1311":"Names from a hat give everyone an equal chance: random sampling.","1313":"Opportunity sampling uses whoever's available, so it's quick and cheap.","1314":"Random sampling needs a list of everyone and can take a long time.","1315":"If the list has a pattern, every nth person could all be similar.","1316":"You need to know the proportions in the population, which takes time.","1317":"Lying about the aim is deception.","1318":"Participants must be allowed to stop, and be protected from distress.","1319":"Publishing names breaks confidentiality.","1320":"A debrief tells participants the truth and offers them the chance to withdraw their data.","1321":"Stop if anyone is distressed, and offer support afterwards.","1322":"Mean: add them up (40) and divide by how many there are (5).","1323":"Median: put them in order and take the middle one.","1324":"Mode: the most common score.","1325":"Range: highest minus lowest (15 − 3).","1326":"Central tendency = an average: mean, median or mode.","1328":"Range = spread from highest to lowest.","1329":"Standard deviation shows spread around the mean.","1332":"Bar charts are for separate categories, so the bars have gaps.","1333":"Histograms are for continuous data, so the bars touch.","1334":"The conditions (IV) go along the bottom; the scores (DV) go up the side.","1335":"A pilot study is a small practice run to catch problems early.","1336":"A control group gets no IV, as a baseline to compare against.","1337":"The same instructions for everyone means fewer extraneous variables.","1338":"Longitudinal = the same people over a long time.","1339":"Cross-sectional = different groups compared at one point in time.","1340":"Longitudinal studies show how the same people change over time.","1341":"They take years, and people drop out.","1342":"A confederate is an actor working for the researcher.","1343":"Confederates create the situation, e.g. giving wrong answers in Asch's study.","1344":"Asch, Milgram and Piliavin all used actors (confederates).","1345":"Peer review means other experts check a study before it's published.","329":"Social influence is how other people change what we think or do.","330":"Conformity is going along with a group, even when the pressure is only imagined.","333":"Deutsch and Gerard named the two reasons we conform: to fit in (normative) or to be right (informational).","336":"Social factors are about the situation: how many people, whether you're anonymous, and how hard the task is.","337":"Dispositional factors are about the person: their personality and how expert they are.","338":"Asch's line study was in 1951, so the 1950s.","339":"Asch wanted to see if people would give an obviously wrong answer to match the group.","340":"Asch's study took place in the USA.","341":"Asch only used male American students, which limits generalisability.","342":"The confederates gave the same wrong answer out loud on critical trials, to see if the real participant would copy.","343":"Asch used 7–8 actors and one real participant in each group.","344":"There were 18 trials; on 12 (the critical ones) the confederates were wrong on purpose.","345":"On average, participants conformed on about a third (36.8%) of critical trials.","346":"Three quarters (75%) conformed at least once.","347":"A quarter (25%) never conformed.","348":"Only 5% conformed on every critical trial.","349":"Conformity jumped with 3 confederates, then levelled off: a bigger group didn't add much.","351":"When unsure, people look to others for the answer (informational influence).","354":"Standardised lab procedures mean Asch's study can be repeated: reliable.","355":"Judging lines isn't like everyday decisions, so it lacks mundane realism.","356":"Participants were told it was a vision test, which is deception.","357":"White, male American students aren't typical of everyone.","359":"Two-tailed predicts a difference, not which condition will have more wrong answers.","360":"Conformity research aims to apply to people in general.","361":"Locus of control was developed by Julian Rotter (1966).","362":"Internal = 'I control what happens to me.'","363":"External = 'Luck or other people control what happens to me.'","364":"If you think others control things, you're more likely to go along with them.","365":"People with an external locus of control agreed with the confederate's cartoon ratings more.","366":"Low confidence and needing approval make people more likely to go along with the group.","367":"Expertise means skill or knowledge in an area.","368":"Confident experts trust their own answer, so they conform less.","369":"People copy an expert because they assume the expert knows best.","370":"Harder problems and low confidence led to more conformity.","371":"Obedience means following a direct order from authority.","372":"Conformity is pressure from peers; obedience is an order from someone in charge.","373":"Milgram wanted to know if ordinary people would obey an order to harm someone.","374":"Participants were told it was about punishment and learning, which hid the real aim.","375":"40 men with a range of ages and jobs answered a newspaper advert: a volunteer sample.","376":"Milgram's study took place at Yale University, a prestigious setting.","377":"The learner, Mr Wallace, was an actor (a confederate).","378":"The draw was rigged, so the real participant was always the teacher.","379":"The teacher had to give 'shocks' rising from 15V to 450V for wrong answers.","380":"Every participant went to at least 300V.","381":"65% went all the way to 450V.","382":"Only 5 participants (12.5%) stopped at 300V.","383":"Sweating, trembling and nervous laughter showed how stressful it was.","384":"Obedience came from the situation and the authority figure, not personality.","385":"Deception, distress and pressure not to withdraw are all ethical problems.","386":"Standardised procedures and prods mean it can be repeated.","387":"Only men took part, so it may not apply to women.","388":"If participants guessed the shocks were fake, they weren't really obeying.","389":"Social factors come from outside the person: agency, culture and proximity.","390":"In the agentic state, people act for an authority and feel the authority is responsible.","394":"Moral strain is the anxiety of doing something you think is wrong.","395":"A social hierarchy is a ranking of authority, like headteacher > teacher > student.","396":"We learn to respect authority from our culture and upbringing.","397":"A run-down office seemed less legitimate than Yale, so obedience fell.","398":"Being closer to the victim increased moral strain, so obedience fell.","399":"Orders by phone carried less authority, so obedience fell.","400":"A uniform signals authority, so people obey more.","401":"Symbols like a uniform, setting or title make authority seem legitimate.","403":"The authoritarian personality is Adorno's (1950) theory.","404":"The authoritarian personality: very respectful of authority, harsh to those of lower status.","405":"Adorno linked strict, harsh parenting to an authoritarian personality.","406":"Adorno's F-scale (fascism scale) measured authoritarian personality.","407":"The F-scale was flawed and the research only shows a link, not cause.","408":"Milgram = situation; Adorno = personality.","409":"A bystander is someone who sees an emergency.","410":"Intervention = stepping in to help.","411":"Apathy = not helping.","413":"Pluralistic ignorance: everyone looks calm, so everyone assumes it isn't an emergency.","414":"Kitty Genovese's murder sparked research into why bystanders don't help.","415":"People alone reported the smoke far more often than people with others who ignored it.","416":"Piliavin's study was 'Good Samaritanism: an underground phenomenon?' (1969).","417":"It took place on the New York subway, a real-life setting.","418":"A real setting with the IV changed by the researchers: a field experiment.","419":"The two conditions were a victim with a cane or a victim who appeared drunk.","420":"Piliavin recorded whether people helped, how fast, and who helped.","421":"The cane victim was helped 95% of the time.","422":"The 'drunk' victim was helped 50% of the time.","423":"Men helped more, and a busy train didn't stop people helping.","424":"One-tailed predicts a direction: the cane victim gets more help.","425":"Real passengers behaved naturally, so the study is high in ecological validity.","426":"Passengers didn't know they were in a study, so there was no consent.","427":"Attitudes have changed since 1969, so the results may be dated.","428":"Social factors come from the situation: other people and the cost of helping.","429":"Dispositional factors come from the person: similarity to the victim and expertise.","431":"If helping costs more than it's worth, people help less.","432":"We feel more empathy for people like us.","433":"Fans helped someone in their own team's shirt more: similarity.","434":"Knowing what to do makes people more confident to help.","435":"Nurses helped more because of their expertise.","436":"Antisocial behaviour is harmful or disrespectful to others.","437":"Pro-social behaviour helps others.","438":"Collective behaviour is how people act as a group, which can differ from how they act alone.","439":"A crowd is a large, temporary group with a shared focus.","440":"Social factors come from the group: deindividuation, social loafing and culture.","441":"Dispositional factors come from the person: personality and morality.","443":"Big groups, anonymity and uniforms make people lose their sense of self.","445":"Hooded, anonymous participants gave twice as many shocks.","446":"Social loafing: putting in less effort in a group than alone.","447":"In tug-of-war, each person pulled less hard as the team got bigger.","448":"People shouted less loudly as the group got bigger.","449":"Individualist cultures put 'me' first, e.g. the UK and USA.","450":"Collectivist cultures put 'us' first, e.g. China and Korea.","451":"Individualist cultures focus on the self, so effort drops more in groups.","452":"Chinese participants tried equally hard either way; Americans only when identifiable.","453":"A group-focused culture may discourage harmful crowd behaviour.","454":"An external locus of control makes people more swayed by the crowd.","455":"Strong morals help people resist the crowd.","456":"Sophie Scholl stood up to the Nazis despite the danger: strong morality.","457":"The three named Social influence studies are Asch, Milgram and Piliavin.","1022":"Authority is the right (or perceived right) to give orders.","1024":"Bystander behaviour is how people react when someone needs help.","1025":"Dispositional factors are personal characteristics.","1026":"Majority influence: the bigger group sways the smaller one, which leads to conformity.","1242":"A harder task means more copying: task difficulty.","1243":"In secret, there's no pressure to fit in: anonymity.","1244":"Blaming outside forces shows an external locus of control.","1245":"Medical skill makes him likely to help: expertise.","1246":"Everyone assumes someone else will help: diffusion of responsibility.","1247":"No one reacts, so everyone assumes it's fine: pluralistic ignorance.","1248":"Helping someone like you: similarity.","1249":"Fear of getting hurt is the cost of helping.","1250":"Less effort in a group is social loafing.","1251":"Matching shirts make fans anonymous: deindividuation.","1253":"The authority figure is further away, so obedience drops: proximity.","1254":"Strong personal values help people resist the crowd: morality.","1255":"Strict parents → authoritarian personality, according to Adorno.","1256":"The IV was what Asch changed: whether the confederates were wrong (or group size).","1257":"The DV was how often the participant gave the wrong answer.","1258":"A controlled lab setting: a laboratory experiment.","1259":"In the variations, the IV was the situational factor changed.","1260":"The DV was the highest voltage given.","1261":"It's a controlled observation of obedience in a lab.","1262":"Milgram used newspaper adverts, so a volunteer sample.","1263":"A range of ages and jobs, but volunteers may be more obedient.","1264":"Debriefing and participants' later approval are Milgram's defence of the deception.","1265":"The IV was the type of victim: cane or drunk.","1266":"Whoever was on the train at the time: opportunity sampling.","1267":"Around 4,450 passengers made a large, varied sample.","1268":"Lower the cost of helping and train people in first aid.","1269":"Sitting in a smoke-filled room isn't a normal situation.","1270":"Knowing about diffusion of responsibility helps people step in.","1271":"Burger and Cooper found people with an external locus of control conformed more.","1272":"People can be internal in some situations and external in others.","1273":"Rating cartoons is trivial, so it lacks mundane realism.","1274":"Agency theory helps explain why people followed destructive orders in the Holocaust.","1275":"Agency theory can't explain the 35% who disobeyed.","1276":"Strict upbringing and high F-scale scores were linked to obedience.","1277":"Adorno measured, but didn't manipulate, so it's correlational.","1278":"Cameras reduce anonymity, which reduces deindividuation.","1279":"Zimbardo's controlled study showed anonymity increased shocks.","1280":"Believing the shocks were real could cause distress.","1281":"If individual effort can be seen, people try harder.","1282":"Noise was measured objectively in a controlled lab.","1283":"Only male students, so it may not apply to women.","1284":"Whole cultures differed, which suggests social norms matter more than personality.","1285":"People within one culture still differ from each other.","1286":"Morality explains why some people resist antisocial crowds.","1287":"Morality is hard to measure objectively.","1288":"Two-tailed predicts a difference, not which way.","1289":"Teach people to question unethical orders."};
